The Goulsse Century

A Retroactive History of the United States, United Kingdom, European Union, Russia, Africa, and China Adopting Gʋlse as a Co-Official Script

Prologue: The Script That Crossed the Atlantic

Historians would later call the period between 2022 and 2045 the Goulsse Transformation.

At its beginning, Gʋlse—more commonly called Goulsse in English—was a young African writing system created for Gur languages, particularly Mooré and Kasem. Its creators envisioned it as more than an alphabet: it was an attempt to place African cultural design at the center of modern digital writing.

The script was distinctive. Geometric, left-to-right, without uppercase and lowercase distinctions, and containing roughly thirty letters, it drew inspiration from Kassena visual traditions while being designed for contemporary linguistic use. (Endangered Alphabets)

At first, almost nobody imagined that it would eventually appear beside Latin, Cyrillic, and Chinese characters on government documents around the world.

But history rarely announces its revolutions in advance.

