I. 2022–2025: The African Script Renaissance

The first Goulsse schools appeared in Burkina Faso.

Students in Pô began learning to read and write Kassem in Gʋlse, while advocates promoted its use for Mooré and eventually other Gur languages. The script’s development attracted attention because it combined indigenous African inspiration with the requirements of modern digital communication. (Unicode)

The movement quickly acquired a philosophical slogan:

“A modern Africa should write itself.”

The phrase became particularly popular among African students and members of the diaspora.

Rather than presenting Goulsse as a replacement for Latin, its advocates presented it as an additional instrument—a script through which African languages could enter the digital world without being dependent exclusively on writing conventions inherited from European colonial administration.

The idea spread.

By 2025, Goulsse educational initiatives had appeared in several African countries, while African technology companies began experimenting with keyboards, fonts, translation software, and digital typography.

The Goulsse movement was still small.

But it had acquired something more important than size:

international attention.

