II. 2026–2029: The American Goulsse Movement

The transformation began in the United States.

African-American universities, African diaspora organizations, linguistics departments, and technology companies began studying Goulsse as an example of contemporary African script development.

The first American debate was not about replacing English’s Latin alphabet.

It was about recognition.

Advocates argued that American schools already taught students multiple writing systems—Latin, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Cyrillic—so there was no reason an African-developed script could not be taught alongside them.

In 2027, several American universities created Goulsse Studies programs.

By 2028, the first American public schools began offering Goulsse as an elective.

Then Silicon Valley became interested.

Engineers developed improved digital fonts, keyboards and translation systems. Goulsse entered social media platforms, educational software and eventually operating systems.

The movement’s most consequential development came when major American universities began using Goulsse in African-language research.

The script stopped being regarded merely as an African cultural experiment.

It became a global writing technology.

