III. 2030: The United States Makes Its Historic Declaration

In 2030, after years of debate, Congress passed the fictional American Script Recognition Act.

The law did not abolish the Latin alphabet.

English remained overwhelmingly written in Latin.

Instead, the United States recognized Gʋlse as a federally recognized co-official script for designated cultural, educational, linguistic and governmental purposes.

Federal agencies were instructed to make certain documents available in both Latin and Goulsse when they concerned African languages, African-American cultural heritage, or federally recognized linguistic programs.

The White House issued its first ceremonial bilingual inscription:

English in Latin.
African-language text in Gʋlse.

The symbolic impact was enormous.

The United States had effectively declared that its definition of national literacy could contain more than the historical alphabet inherited from Europe.

