IV. 2031–2034: Britain Joins the Transformation

Britain initially watched the American experiment cautiously.

The United Kingdom had an especially complicated relationship with the issue.

English had become the world’s dominant international language, but Britain was also home to enormous African and Caribbean communities.

Universities in London, Birmingham, Manchester and Edinburgh began establishing African writing-system research centers.

In 2032, Parliament passed the Cultural Scripts Recognition Act.

Gʋlse became an officially recognized supplementary script for government cultural institutions and educational programs.

London Underground stations hosting major African cultural districts began displaying selected announcements in Goulsse.

The British Museum opened a permanent exhibition titled:

“Africa Writes: From Ancient Symbols to Gʋlse.”

The exhibition became one of the most visited linguistic exhibitions in Britain.

By 2034, Goulsse had become a familiar sight in London.

It appeared on university signs.

On cultural festivals.

On museum exhibits.

On African-language publications.

And occasionally, beside English, on government documents.

