V. 2035: The European Union’s Goulsse Charter

The European Union approached Goulsse differently.

Rather than adopting it as a single replacement script, the EU treated it as part of its growing philosophy of multilingual and multicultural digital citizenship.

The European Parliament established a commission to study African writing systems and digital linguistic inclusion.

In 2035, the EU adopted the fictional Gʋlse Cultural and Digital Accessibility Charter.

The charter recognized Goulsse as an official supplementary script of the European Union for cultural, educational and linguistic purposes.

EU institutions began commissioning Goulsse versions of selected documents dealing with African languages, migration, cultural heritage and international development.

Brussels became the center of European Goulsse scholarship.

Paris became the center of Goulsse publishing.

Berlin became the center of Goulsse technology.

And Lisbon became a major hub for Lusophone African Goulsse projects.

The European Union had not abandoned Latin.

Instead, it had formally accepted the idea that one civilization could possess multiple legitimate writing traditions simultaneously.

