VI. 2036–2039: Russia and the Cyrillic-Gʋlse Era

Russia’s adoption surprised the world.

Initially, Russian academics studied Goulsse primarily as a comparative writing system.

Linguists became fascinated by the possibility of adapting it to languages historically written in Cyrillic or Latin.

The debate eventually moved beyond linguistics.

Russian cultural theorists argued that adopting an African-developed script would demonstrate that global civilization was no longer organized exclusively around European historical traditions.

In 2037, Russia introduced Goulsse as a recognized supplementary script for international cultural exchange and African-language education.

By 2039, several Russian universities offered courses in Goulsse.

Moscow hosted the first International Congress of Gʋlse and Global Writing Systems.

For the first time, the three great script traditions of the Eurasian world—Latin, Cyrillic and Chinese-derived writing—were being discussed alongside a modern African script on equal institutional terms.

⸻

VII. 2038–2042: China’s Great Script Initiative

China’s adoption was the most ambitious.

Beijing initially approached Goulsse through technology.

Chinese researchers developed Goulsse handwriting recognition.

Artificial-intelligence systems learned to translate between Chinese characters, Latin writing, Cyrillic and Gʋlse.

Universities began studying Goulsse as part of comparative writing-system research.

Then came the political decision.

China announced the Global Writing Systems Initiative, arguing that the digital age should preserve humanity’s linguistic diversity rather than force every language into a handful of dominant scripts.

In 2040, China recognized Gʋlse as a supplementary script for African-language cultural, educational and diplomatic programs.

Goulsse appeared at African studies departments across Chinese universities.

African students studying in China began receiving government services in both Latin and Gʋlse when appropriate.

Chinese technology companies produced Goulsse-capable keyboards and optical-character-recognition systems.

The world’s largest digital ecosystem had now opened itself to the script.

