VIII. 2040–2045: Africa Reclaims the Center

The most important consequence occurred not in Washington, London, Brussels, Moscow or Beijing.

It occurred in Africa.

African governments increasingly recognized that Goulsse did not have to be imposed as a universal replacement for Latin, Arabic, or existing indigenous scripts.

Instead, it could become part of a plural African writing ecosystem.

Burkina Faso became the symbolic birthplace of the movement.

Nigeria became its technological powerhouse.

Ghana became a center for linguistic scholarship.

Senegal developed educational applications.

South Africa incorporated Goulsse into selected African-language cultural programs.

Kenya and Tanzania experimented with adaptations for local-language education.

Across the continent, a new principle emerged:

No African language should be considered technologically backward because of the script in which it is written.

Goulsse became one of the instruments through which that principle was implemented.

