IX. The Six-Script World

By 2045, historians described the new global writing environment as the Six-Script World.

Latin remained dominant internationally.

Chinese characters remained central to East Asian civilization.

Cyrillic continued to serve Russian and numerous other languages.

Arabic remained fundamental across much of the Islamic world.

And Gʋlse emerged as a globally recognized African writing system.

But something deeper had changed.

Scripts were no longer understood simply as technical mechanisms for recording speech.

They had become cultural sovereignties.

The appearance of Goulsse on an American university certificate did not mean America had become African.

The appearance of Goulsse in a Chinese university did not mean China had abandoned Chinese characters.

The appearance of Goulsse in Moscow did not mean Cyrillic had disappeared.

Instead, the world had accepted a new proposition:

Civilizations could share scripts without surrendering their identities.

