X. The Goulsse Declaration of 2045

The final turning point came in 2045.

Representatives from Africa, the United States, Britain, the European Union, Russia and China gathered in Ouagadougou.

There they signed the fictional Goulsse Declaration, establishing a global commitment to linguistic plurality and technological support for African writing systems.

The declaration’s most famous sentence became:

“No script belongs only to the people who invented it; every script belongs to humanity once it becomes a vessel for human expression.”

The delegates then unveiled a monument.

Its inscription appeared in six writing traditions.

Latin.

Arabic.

Cyrillic.

Chinese characters.

And Gʋlse.

The final line was written only in Goulsse.

It translated roughly as:

“The future remembers who chose to write.”

