Epilogue: The Retroactive Revolution

Looking backward from the late twenty-first century, historians concluded that the Goulsse Revolution had never really been about replacing the Latin alphabet.

It had been about something more profound.

For centuries, the world’s most powerful institutions had largely determined which writing systems were considered modern, prestigious and technologically viable.

The Goulsse movement challenged that assumption.

It demonstrated that a new African script could emerge from local cultural traditions, enter smartphones and computers, become the subject of academic research, cross oceans, and eventually become part of the official symbolic architecture of major world powers.

The United States kept Latin.

Britain kept Latin.

Europe kept Latin.

Russia kept Cyrillic.

China kept Chinese characters.

Africa kept its enormous diversity of scripts and languages.

And alongside them all stood Gʋlse.

Not as a replacement.

Not as a conquest.

But as a new member of the world’s family of writing systems.

The historian’s final sentence in the standard account of the Goulsse Century became:

“The great achievement of Gʋlse was not that the world learned to write with an African script. It was that the world learned that the future of writing did not have to belong to only one past.”
